  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Welcome, Maths Detective!"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "This is a **check-in**, not a test. We want to find out what you already know so your next lessons fit you perfectly. In the lesson you can have a go, peek at hints and explore. Keep paper and a pencil nearby for working out."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "emoji": "🔍",
      "alt": "Detective mode on!",
      "caption": "Detective mode on!"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 1: Place value and big numbers"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "Each digit has a value that depends on its place. In **4,825** the 4 is worth 4,000, the 8 is worth 800, the 2 is worth 20 and the 5 is worth 5. We can **round** a number to the nearest 10, 100 or 1,000 to make it easier to work with."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is the value of the 7 in 3,742?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 700,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "Round 3,742 to the nearest hundred.",
        "hint": "Look at the tens digit: 4 means round down.",
        "answer": {
          "value": 3700,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 2: Times tables and division"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "Times tables are the building blocks of multiplication and division. If you know 6 x 7 = 42, you also know 42 / 7 = 6 and 42 / 6 = 7. These are called a **fact family**."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is 8 x 6?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 48,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is 54 / 9?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 6,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "activity",
      "title": "Fact family race",
      "materials": [
        "paper",
        "pencil",
        "timer"
      ],
      "instructions": [
        "Pick a times table fact, for example 7 x 8 = 56.",
        "Write the three other facts in its family.",
        "Set a 2 minute timer and see how many families you can write.",
        "Which table was trickiest? Note it down."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 3: Adding and taking away big numbers"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "To add or subtract big numbers, line up the digits in columns: ones under ones, tens under tens. Remember to **carry** when a column makes 10 or more, and to **borrow** when you need more."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is 468 + 357?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 825,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is 1,000 - 365?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 635,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 4: Fractions"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "A **fraction** is part of a whole. In 3/4 the bottom number (4) says how many equal parts, and the top number (3) says how many we have. Fractions like 1/2 and 2/4 are **equivalent**: they are the same amount."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "multiple_choice",
        "prompt": "Which fraction is equal to 1/2?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "2/3"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "2/4"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "1/4"
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          "b"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "vocab",
      "word": "denominator",
      "meaning": "The bottom number of a fraction. It tells you how many equal parts the whole is split into.",
      "example": "In 3/5 the denominator is 5."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 5: Shapes and measuring"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "The **perimeter** of a shape is the distance all the way around its edges. For a rectangle, add up all four sides. The **area** is the space inside, which you find by multiplying length by width."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "A rectangle is 6 cm long and 3 cm wide. What is its perimeter in cm?",
        "hint": "6 + 3 + 6 + 3",
        "answer": {
          "value": 18,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "reveal",
      "prompt": "A garden is 5 m by 4 m. What is its area?",
      "answer_markdown": "5 x 4 = 20 square metres."
    },
    {
      "type": "summary",
      "points": [
        "Place value tells us what each digit is worth.",
        "Times tables and division belong together in fact families.",
        "Column methods help with big adding and taking away.",
        "Equivalent fractions are the same amount written differently.",
        "Perimeter goes around a shape, area fills it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "style": "tip",
      "markdown": "When you are ready, start the quiz. It has 12 questions and takes about 15 minutes. Do your best. If you do not know one, have a go anyway: it helps us plan."
    }
  ],
